{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H4Gv7Kqk7M3pqgm4QnxcpS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Review: ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Focus: Novelty and Related Work**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThe paper introduces ProgressProbe, a system that detects \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories using a two-stage pipeline: (1) three hand-crafted labeling functions aggregated via majority vote to produce weak labels, and (2) a small GRU+attention probe trained on these labels. The approach is evaluated on SWE-bench trajectories from SWE-agent and OpenHands, showing strong macro-F1 and cross-agent transfer. The framing combines data programming (Snorkel) with probing classifiers, applied to the agent monitoring domain.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Problem identification is practically valuable.** Semantic stagnation — agents that appear active but make no real progress — is a genuine pain point in deployed agent systems. Naming and operationalizing this failure mode is useful, even if the underlying concept is not entirely new.\n\n**S2. The weak-supervision-to-probe pipeline is well-motivated.** The observation that majority-vote labels from domain-specific LFs can train a temporal model that *surpasses* the LFs themselves (+4.43pp over LF oracle) is a clean and convincing result. This \"student exceeds teacher\" effect, attributed to the GRU capturing inter-step temporal dependencies the i.i.d. LFs miss, is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution.\n\n**S3. Cross-agent transfer is a meaningful evaluation.** Showing that a probe trained on SWE-agent trajectories retains 0.835 F1 on OpenHands trajectories demonstrates that the learned signal is not merely an artifact of one agent's idiosyncratic behavior patterns. This is the kind of generalization test that strengthens practical claims.\n\n**S4. Cost-efficiency comparison is well-grounded.** The 31x cost reduction over Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot, with superior accuracy, gives the work immediate practical relevance regardless of novelty debates.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The novelty is thin — both components are straightforward applications of existing techniques.** The paper's two claimed contributions decompose into: (a) applying Snorkel's majority-vote aggregation to trajectory data with three hand-written heuristics, and (b) training a GRU classifier. Neither component involves methodological innovation. The labeling functions (file diff delta, command repetition, outcome backpropagation) are reasonable engineering choices but not technically novel — they are essentially threshold-based heuristics. The GRU+attention architecture is standard. The claim of being \"first to apply data programming to sequential trajectory data\" is a narrow priority claim rather than a conceptual advance; the extension from i.i.d. to sequential is handled entirely by the downstream GRU, not by any modification to the data programming framework itself.\n\n**W2. The related work is severely incomplete — 10 references is far below the standard for a top venue.** A typical top-venue paper in this area would cite 30-50 references. Critical missing lines of work include:\n\n- **Process Reward Models (PRMs) and Outcome Reward Models (ORMs):** Lightman et al. (2023) \"Let's Verify Step by Step,\" Wang et al. (2024) \"Math-Shepherd,\" and the broader ORM/PRM literature are directly relevant. These models estimate step-level correctness/progress in reasoning chains — precisely the same conceptual task as ProgressProbe but in mathematical reasoning. The paper never acknowledges or differentiates from this line of work, which is a serious omission.\n\n- **Value functions in agent RL:** Any RL-based agent framework learns a value function that estimates expected future reward — this is literally a progress estimator. The connection to temporal-difference learning and advantage estimation is unacknowledged. Works on training value functions for code agents (e.g., CodeRL, RLHF-based agent training) should be discussed.\n\n- **Agent scaffolding with self-reflection:** Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023), LATS (Zhou et al., 2023), and related tree-search/self-critique methods detect when agents are failing and intervene. These are closely related intervention mechanisms that the paper ignores entirely.\n\n- **Execution feedback and test-based progress:** SWE-bench itself and follow-up works (Jimenez et al., 2024) use test execution as a progress signal. The relationship between ProgressProbe's file_diff_delta LF and test-pass-rate signals needs discussion.\n\n- **Trajectory-level anomaly detection in agents:** Beyond the two papers cited (Chen 2024a, Guo 2024), there is growing work on detecting agent failure modes, including ReGAL (Liang et al.), AgentBench analysis, and various agent evaluation frameworks.\n\n- **Weak supervision beyond Snorkel:** The data programming literature has advanced substantially since Ratner et al. 2017/2019 — works on learning LF accuracy (MeTaL, FlyingSquid), programmatic labeling for NLP (Zheng et al., 2022), and applications to structured prediction should be discussed.\n\n**W3. \"Semantic stagnation\" may be a rebranding of known concepts.** The RL literature has long studied \"plateaus\" in learning curves; the agent literature discusses \"looping\" and \"unproductive exploration.\" The paper's Chen et al. (2024a) citation on cycle detection already addresses a related phenomenon. The paper claims semantic stagnation is distinct because the agent's *actions* are diverse even though *progress* stalls — but this is essentially the exploration-exploitation failure mode, well-studied in RL. The paper does not adequately argue why this requires a new term rather than positioning within the existing vocabulary.\n\n**W4. The comparison to LLM-as-judge is underspecified.** The Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot baseline is a single configuration. The LLM-as-judge literature (Zheng et al., 2023) shows that prompt engineering, chain-of-thought, and multi-turn evaluation substantially affect LLM judgment quality. Testing one prompt template against one model does not constitute a thorough comparison with the LLM-based monitoring paradigm.\n\n**W5. Domain specificity undermines generality claims.** The three LFs are hand-crafted for SWE-bench-style coding agents (file diffs, shell commands, test outcomes). The paper does not discuss how the framework would transfer to non-coding agent tasks (web browsing, tool use, general reasoning), which limits the contribution's scope. This domain specificity also raises the venue-fit question: the problem and solution are deeply embedded in software engineering agent evaluation, yet the paper targets an NLP/ML venue without adequately justifying why this is an NLP contribution rather than an SE one.\n\n**W6. The evaluation is confined to a single task domain.** Both evaluation settings (SWE-agent and OpenHands) operate on SWE-bench GitHub issues. Cross-agent transfer within the same task distribution is a weaker form of generalization than cross-task transfer. Without evaluation on a different agent task (e.g., WebArena, ToolBench, or mathematical reasoning agents), the generality of the approach is undemonstrated.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n1. How does ProgressProbe differ from training a Process Reward Model (PRM) on agent trajectories? Both estimate step-level quality along a trajectory. If the distinction is supervision source (weak labels vs. human annotations), this should be explicitly discussed and compared.\n\n2. Could a simple value function trained with TD learning on trajectory returns achieve similar detection performance? What is the advantage of the weak-supervision pipeline over standard RL reward prediction?\n\n3. The three LFs are specific to coding agents. What is the concrete plan for extending to non-coding domains? Would the entire LF set need to be redesigned, and if so, does the framework contribute anything beyond \"write heuristics, train a classifier\"?\n\n4. Why were only 10 references included? This appears to be well below the expected thoroughness for a venue submission. Were the PRM/ORM, Reflexion/LATS, and RL value function literatures considered and deliberately excluded?\n\n5. The GRU probe operates on trajectory embeddings — what embedder is used, and how sensitive are results to the embedding model choice? This seems like a critical design decision that is underspecified.\n\n6. At 1.32M parameters, the probe is small. Was a simple logistic regression or random forest on handcrafted features (LF outputs + basic trajectory statistics) tried as a baseline? The mean-pool+LR baseline may not represent the best non-temporal alternative.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- Section 2 is only three short paragraphs covering three broad areas. For a venue submission, each area warrants deeper treatment with finer-grained positioning.\n- The Fleiss kappa of 0.724 for LF agreement is reported as a quality metric, but this measures inter-LF agreement, not agreement with ground truth. The 84.5% accuracy against human labels is the correct quality metric; the kappa statistic is misleading as presented.\n- The \"31x lower cost\" claim depends on assumptions about API pricing that will change rapidly. This should be presented more carefully, perhaps as order-of-magnitude rather than a precise multiplier.\n- The term \"probe\" in the title and throughout the paper carries specific connotations from the probing literature (Belinkov, 2022) — probes are diagnostic tools for analyzing representations, not deployed classifiers. Using the term for a deployed monitoring system is technically a misnomer and may confuse readers.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 3/5 | The pipeline works and results are internally consistent, but the evaluation is narrow (single task domain) and key baselines (PRMs, RL value functions) are missing. |\n| **Presentation** | 3/5 | Writing is clear and the pipeline is well-explained, but the related work section is inadequate for a venue submission. |\n| **Contribution** | 2/5 | The individual components (Snorkel + GRU) are not novel; the combination is straightforward engineering. The related work gaps mean the paper fails to adequately position its contribution against the most relevant prior art (PRMs, value functions, agent self-reflection). |\n| **Overall** | 3/6 (Reject) | The practical utility is real, but novelty is insufficient for a top venue. The paper reads as a well-executed engineering contribution — applying known techniques (data programming, recurrent probes) to a specific domain (SWE-bench agent monitoring) — rather than a methodological advance. The severely incomplete related work section is the most actionable issue: the authors must engage with PRMs, RL value functions, and agent self-reflection methods to credibly claim novelty. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I am familiar with the data programming, agent monitoring, and process reward model literatures. My uncertainty is primarily around whether unpublished concurrent work may have addressed similar gaps. |\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation\n\n**Reject.** The paper addresses a real problem and the pipeline achieves strong empirical results, but the novelty bar for a top venue is not met. The two-stage approach (heuristic labeling functions + GRU classifier) is a competent application of existing methods, not a methodological contribution. The related work section, with only 10 references and no engagement with process reward models, RL value functions, or agent self-reflection methods, is substantially below the expected standard. To strengthen the paper: (1) deeply engage with the PRM/ORM and RL value estimation literatures, clearly articulating what semantic stagnation detection adds beyond step-level reward prediction; (2) evaluate on at least one non-coding agent domain; (3) compare against a PRM baseline and a simple value-function baseline; (4) expand the related work to 30+ references with fine-grained positioning."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac0f53bf7c65a7c16 (use SendMessage with to: 'ac0f53bf7c65a7c16' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 17907\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 70804</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37dd1f71-0479-4eaf-8dec-0b477d2b7a27","uuid":"39164126-6549-436e-a311-17b72fec92ea","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:52:55.239Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate the paper \"ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\" using the 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; acceptance threshold ~25%).\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper proposes ProgressProbe, detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — agents executing diverse but collectively ineffective actions. Two contributions:\n1. A data-programming pipeline transferring Snorkel's paradigm to sequential agent trajectories, using 3 domain-specific labeling functions (file_diff_delta, command_repetition, outcome_backpropagation). Their majority vote achieves 84.5% accuracy vs human gold labels (Fleiss κ=0.724).\n2. A lightweight GRU probe with attention (1.32M params) trained on these noisy labels, achieving 0.91 macro-F1 on MV labels and 0.86 on human gold set. It beats LF oracle by +4.43pp and Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot by +3.74pp at 31x lower cost. Cross-agent transfer SWE-agent→OpenHands retains 0.835 F1.\n\n## Related Work (from paper, Section 2)\nThree short paragraphs:\n1. **Agent monitoring and intervention**: Chen et al.(2024a) cycle detection, Guo et al.(2024) trajectory anomaly, Luo et al.(2024)/Chen et al.(2024b) reward models. Paper claims these miss semantic stagnation or lose window-level temporal dynamics.\n2. **Data programming and weak supervision**: Snorkel (Ratner et al. 2017, 2019). Claims to be first to apply data programming to sequential trajectory data where LF temporal ordering matters.\n3. **Probing learned representations**: Belinkov (2022). Claims novelty in using probes for deployment rather than analysis.\n\nReferences total: 8 (Yang 2024, Wang 2024, Chen 2024a, Guo 2024, Luo 2024, Chen 2024b, Zhang 2024, Ratner 2017, Ratner 2019, Belinkov 2022) — actually 10 references.\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Weak-supervision framework successfully transfers Snorkel to sequential trajectory domain (non-trivial: LFs exhibit temporal dynamics absent in i.i.d. settings)\n2. Temporal modeling matters: GRU probe learns richer signals than the LFs that trained it (+4.43pp over LF oracle, +13.72pp over mean-pool+LR)\n3. Cross-agent generalization: learned progress signal transfers across agent implementations (SWE-agent→OpenHands)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty + related work focus, numbered)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty + related work focus, numbered)\n4. **Questions to Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Presentation (1-5), Contribution (1-5), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Focus on:\n- Is the novelty sufficient for a top venue? Is \"applying Snorkel to trajectories + training a GRU\" novel enough?\n- Is the related work coverage adequate? Missing comparisons?\n- Are there closely related works not cited? (Consider: process reward models like Math-Shepherd, ORM/PRM literature, RL-based value functions for agents, agent scaffolding works like Reflexion/LATS, execution feedback mechanisms)\n- Is the \"semantic stagnation\" framing genuinely new or a rebranding of existing concepts?\n- How does this compare to step-level reward models (ORM/PRM) which also estimate progress?\n- The paper has only 10 references — is this sufficient?\n- Venue fit: the paper targets NLP/ML but the problem is arguably more SE. How well is the NLP angle justified?\n- Is the contribution primarily methodological (framework) or empirical (showing it works)?\n\nBe specific about what related work is missing and how it would change the paper's positioning.\n","agentId":"ac0f53bf7c65a7c16","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Review: ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\n**Reviewer 2 — Focus: Novelty and Related Work**\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThe paper introduces ProgressProbe, a system that detects \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories using a two-stage pipeline: (1) three hand-crafted labeling functions aggregated via majority vote to produce weak labels, and (2) a small GRU+attention probe trained on these labels. The approach is evaluated on SWE-bench trajectories from SWE-agent and OpenHands, showing strong macro-F1 and cross-agent transfer. The framing combines data programming (Snorkel) with probing classifiers, applied to the agent monitoring domain.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Problem identification is practically valuable.** Semantic stagnation — agents that appear active but make no real progress — is a genuine pain point in deployed agent systems. Naming and operationalizing this failure mode is useful, even if the underlying concept is not entirely new.\n\n**S2. The weak-supervision-to-probe pipeline is well-motivated.** The observation that majority-vote labels from domain-specific LFs can train a temporal model that *surpasses* the LFs themselves (+4.43pp over LF oracle) is a clean and convincing result. This \"student exceeds teacher\" effect, attributed to the GRU capturing inter-step temporal dependencies the i.i.d. LFs miss, is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution.\n\n**S3. Cross-agent transfer is a meaningful evaluation.** Showing that a probe trained on SWE-agent trajectories retains 0.835 F1 on OpenHands trajectories demonstrates that the learned signal is not merely an artifact of one agent's idiosyncratic behavior patterns. This is the kind of generalization test that strengthens practical claims.\n\n**S4. Cost-efficiency comparison is well-grounded.** The 31x cost reduction over Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot, with superior accuracy, gives the work immediate practical relevance regardless of novelty debates.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The novelty is thin — both components are straightforward applications of existing techniques.** The paper's two claimed contributions decompose into: (a) applying Snorkel's majority-vote aggregation to trajectory data with three hand-written heuristics, and (b) training a GRU classifier. Neither component involves methodological innovation. The labeling functions (file diff delta, command repetition, outcome backpropagation) are reasonable engineering choices but not technically novel — they are essentially threshold-based heuristics. The GRU+attention architecture is standard. The claim of being \"first to apply data programming to sequential trajectory data\" is a narrow priority claim rather than a conceptual advance; the extension from i.i.d. to sequential is handled entirely by the downstream GRU, not by any modification to the data programming framework itself.\n\n**W2. The related work is severely incomplete — 10 references is far below the standard for a top venue.** A typical top-venue paper in this area would cite 30-50 references. Critical missing lines of work include:\n\n- **Process Reward Models (PRMs) and Outcome Reward Models (ORMs):** Lightman et al. (2023) \"Let's Verify Step by Step,\" Wang et al. (2024) \"Math-Shepherd,\" and the broader ORM/PRM literature are directly relevant. These models estimate step-level correctness/progress in reasoning chains — precisely the same conceptual task as ProgressProbe but in mathematical reasoning. The paper never acknowledges or differentiates from this line of work, which is a serious omission.\n\n- **Value functions in agent RL:** Any RL-based agent framework learns a value function that estimates expected future reward — this is literally a progress estimator. The connection to temporal-difference learning and advantage estimation is unacknowledged. Works on training value functions for code agents (e.g., CodeRL, RLHF-based agent training) should be discussed.\n\n- **Agent scaffolding with self-reflection:** Reflexion (Shinn et al., 2023), LATS (Zhou et al., 2023), and related tree-search/self-critique methods detect when agents are failing and intervene. These are closely related intervention mechanisms that the paper ignores entirely.\n\n- **Execution feedback and test-based progress:** SWE-bench itself and follow-up works (Jimenez et al., 2024) use test execution as a progress signal. The relationship between ProgressProbe's file_diff_delta LF and test-pass-rate signals needs discussion.\n\n- **Trajectory-level anomaly detection in agents:** Beyond the two papers cited (Chen 2024a, Guo 2024), there is growing work on detecting agent failure modes, including ReGAL (Liang et al.), AgentBench analysis, and various agent evaluation frameworks.\n\n- **Weak supervision beyond Snorkel:** The data programming literature has advanced substantially since Ratner et al. 2017/2019 — works on learning LF accuracy (MeTaL, FlyingSquid), programmatic labeling for NLP (Zheng et al., 2022), and applications to structured prediction should be discussed.\n\n**W3. \"Semantic stagnation\" may be a rebranding of known concepts.** The RL literature has long studied \"plateaus\" in learning curves; the agent literature discusses \"looping\" and \"unproductive exploration.\" The paper's Chen et al. (2024a) citation on cycle detection already addresses a related phenomenon. The paper claims semantic stagnation is distinct because the agent's *actions* are diverse even though *progress* stalls — but this is essentially the exploration-exploitation failure mode, well-studied in RL. The paper does not adequately argue why this requires a new term rather than positioning within the existing vocabulary.\n\n**W4. The comparison to LLM-as-judge is underspecified.** The Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot baseline is a single configuration. The LLM-as-judge literature (Zheng et al., 2023) shows that prompt engineering, chain-of-thought, and multi-turn evaluation substantially affect LLM judgment quality. Testing one prompt template against one model does not constitute a thorough comparison with the LLM-based monitoring paradigm.\n\n**W5. Domain specificity undermines generality claims.** The three LFs are hand-crafted for SWE-bench-style coding agents (file diffs, shell commands, test outcomes). The paper does not discuss how the framework would transfer to non-coding agent tasks (web browsing, tool use, general reasoning), which limits the contribution's scope. This domain specificity also raises the venue-fit question: the problem and solution are deeply embedded in software engineering agent evaluation, yet the paper targets an NLP/ML venue without adequately justifying why this is an NLP contribution rather than an SE one.\n\n**W6. The evaluation is confined to a single task domain.** Both evaluation settings (SWE-agent and OpenHands) operate on SWE-bench GitHub issues. Cross-agent transfer within the same task distribution is a weaker form of generalization than cross-task transfer. Without evaluation on a different agent task (e.g., WebArena, ToolBench, or mathematical reasoning agents), the generality of the approach is undemonstrated.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n1. How does ProgressProbe differ from training a Process Reward Model (PRM) on agent trajectories? Both estimate step-level quality along a trajectory. If the distinction is supervision source (weak labels vs. human annotations), this should be explicitly discussed and compared.\n\n2. Could a simple value function trained with TD learning on trajectory returns achieve similar detection performance? What is the advantage of the weak-supervision pipeline over standard RL reward prediction?\n\n3. The three LFs are specific to coding agents. What is the concrete plan for extending to non-coding domains? Would the entire LF set need to be redesigned, and if so, does the framework contribute anything beyond \"write heuristics, train a classifier\"?\n\n4. Why were only 10 references included? This appears to be well below the expected thoroughness for a venue submission. Were the PRM/ORM, Reflexion/LATS, and RL value function literatures considered and deliberately excluded?\n\n5. The GRU probe operates on trajectory embeddings — what embedder is used, and how sensitive are results to the embedding model choice? This seems like a critical design decision that is underspecified.\n\n6. At 1.32M parameters, the probe is small. Was a simple logistic regression or random forest on handcrafted features (LF outputs + basic trajectory statistics) tried as a baseline? The mean-pool+LR baseline may not represent the best non-temporal alternative.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- Section 2 is only three short paragraphs covering three broad areas. For a venue submission, each area warrants deeper treatment with finer-grained positioning.\n- The Fleiss kappa of 0.724 for LF agreement is reported as a quality metric, but this measures inter-LF agreement, not agreement with ground truth. The 84.5% accuracy against human labels is the correct quality metric; the kappa statistic is misleading as presented.\n- The \"31x lower cost\" claim depends on assumptions about API pricing that will change rapidly. This should be presented more carefully, perhaps as order-of-magnitude rather than a precise multiplier.\n- The term \"probe\" in the title and throughout the paper carries specific connotations from the probing literature (Belinkov, 2022) — probes are diagnostic tools for analyzing representations, not deployed classifiers. Using the term for a deployed monitoring system is technically a misnomer and may confuse readers.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 3/5 | The pipeline works and results are internally consistent, but the evaluation is narrow (single task domain) and key baselines (PRMs, RL value functions) are missing. |\n| **Presentation** | 3/5 | Writing is clear and the pipeline is well-explained, but the related work section is inadequate for a venue submission. |\n| **Contribution** | 2/5 | The individual components (Snorkel + GRU) are not novel; the combination is straightforward engineering. The related work gaps mean the paper fails to adequately position its contribution against the most relevant prior art (PRMs, value functions, agent self-reflection). |\n| **Overall** | 3/6 (Reject) | The practical utility is real, but novelty is insufficient for a top venue. The paper reads as a well-executed engineering contribution — applying known techniques (data programming, recurrent probes) to a specific domain (SWE-bench agent monitoring) — rather than a methodological advance. The severely incomplete related work section is the most actionable issue: the authors must engage with PRMs, RL value functions, and agent self-reflection methods to credibly claim novelty. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I am familiar with the data programming, agent monitoring, and process reward model literatures. My uncertainty is primarily around whether unpublished concurrent work may have addressed similar gaps. |\n\n---\n\n### Summary Recommendation\n\n**Reject.** The paper addresses a real problem and the pipeline achieves strong empirical results, but the novelty bar for a top venue is not met. The two-stage approach (heuristic labeling functions + GRU classifier) is a competent application of existing methods, not a methodological contribution. The related work section, with only 10 references and no engagement with process reward models, RL value functions, or agent self-reflection methods, is substantially below the expected standard. To strengthen the paper: (1) deeply engage with the PRM/ORM and RL value estimation literatures, clearly articulating what semantic stagnation detection adds beyond step-level reward prediction; (2) evaluate on at least one non-coding agent domain; (3) compare against a PRM baseline and a simple value-function baseline; (4) expand the related work to 30+ references with fine-grained positioning."}],"totalDurationMs":70804,"totalTokens":17907,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6843,"cache_read_input_tokens":8247,"output_tokens":2815,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6843},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2815,"cache_read_input_tokens":8247,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6843,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6843,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
