{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013y6GiByD2h9oKVGAZ9ffpa","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its methodology, implementation details, and experimental design. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories -- situations where agents execute diverse but collectively ineffective actions. The authors propose ProgressProbe, consisting of (1) a data-programming pipeline with three labeling functions (file_diff_delta, command_repetition, outcome_backpropagation) that produce window-level progress/stagnation labels via majority vote, and (2) a lightweight GRU probe with attention (1.32M params) trained on these weakly-supervised labels. The paper reports strong results on SWE-bench trajectories: 0.906 macro-F1 on majority-vote labels, 0.862 on a 400-window human gold set, with cross-agent transfer to OpenHands (0.835 F1) and practical downstream value in early termination (32.7% token savings at 96.1% success preservation).\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Comprehensive experimental coverage.** The paper covers an unusually thorough set of experiments for a methods paper: 16 distinct experiments spanning main results, multiple baseline families, ablations across every design axis (window size, architecture, LF composition, loss function, feature type, data scale), error analysis, cross-agent transfer, and a downstream application. This leaves very few obvious experimental gaps.\n\n**S2. Circularity explicitly addressed.** The most obvious threat to validity -- that the probe is trained and evaluated on the same automatically generated labels -- is directly mitigated by the 400-window human-annotated gold set (Fleiss kappa=0.724, substantial agreement). The probe's advantage over baselines holds on human labels (0.862 macro-F1, +6.89pp over Repeat-3), which is the right evaluation to foreground. The paper correctly places this as Table 1.\n\n**S3. Strong baseline suite that includes LLM-as-Judge.** Including Claude Sonnet 4 and GPT-4o in zero-shot and few-shot settings preempts the obvious \"why not just use an LLM?\" question. The cost-accuracy Pareto analysis (31x cheaper, 606x faster, AND higher F1) is convincing. The static aggregation baselines (Mean-pool+LR/SVM, Max-pool+LR) provide clean controls for isolating the contribution of temporal modeling.\n\n**S4. Instance-level data splitting.** The code confirms that train/val/test splits are done by instance_id (not by window), preventing data leakage from windows of the same trajectory appearing in both train and test. This is a critical correctness property that many papers in this space miss.\n\n**S5. Principled separation of method from architecture.** The paper explicitly frames the GRU architecture as a \"deliberate engineering choice, not a contribution\" and supports this with ablations showing GRU and Transformer differ by <1pp. This honest positioning strengthens the methodological contribution (weak-supervision pipeline + empirical study).\n\n**S6. Informative error analysis.** The decomposition into FP (exploratory behavior misclassified as stagnation, 98 cases) and FN (diverse but ineffective commands, 123 cases) identifies meaningful failure modes. The error overlap analysis with Repeat-3 (probe uniquely corrects 187 cases, loses only 34) quantifies the practical advantage precisely.\n\n**S7. LF temporal dynamics analysis.** The observation that labeling functions exhibit leading/lagging behavior at stagnation onset (cmd_rep leads by 1.8 steps, outcome_bp lags by 2.1 steps, agreement rises from 34% to 71%) is a genuinely novel insight about applying data programming to sequential data. This is the most original analytical contribution.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The sentence encoder is not named in the paper.** Section 3.3 says only \"a frozen sentence encoder\" with dimension 1024. The source code reveals this is **Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B** with last-token pooling and L2 normalization, truncated to 512 tokens. This is a serious omission for reproducibility. The choice of embedding model is a critical design decision -- results may differ substantially with, e.g., all-MiniLM-L6 or BGE-large. An embedding model ablation is entirely absent.\n\n**W2. No variance reporting or significance testing across multiple runs.** The experiment plan includes a \"probe-multiseed\" experiment (seeds 42, 123, 456) to confirm the 6.24pp gap is statistically stable, but the paper reports only single-seed results. With margins of 3-7pp over baselines and a relatively small human gold set (400 windows), it is impossible to assess whether the observed gains are within run-to-run variance. This is a standard requirement for ML venues. The training code sets seed=42 with no multi-run support built in.\n\n**W3. The GRU probe lacks the attention mechanism described in the paper.** The paper describes a BiGRU with attention (Equations 2-3, Section 3.4), complete with attention weight analysis (concentration coefficient 0.73, onset localization at steps 2-3). However, the actual `ProgressProbe` class in `probe_model.py` implements a **unidirectional 2-layer GRU that takes the last hidden state** (`hidden[-1]`) followed by a linear classifier -- there is no attention mechanism whatsoever. Either the paper describes a different model than what was actually trained, or the code reviewed here is not the final version. This discrepancy undermines trust in the reported attention-based interpretability results.\n\n**W4. outcome_backpropagation LF introduces label leakage from the future.** This LF propagates the final trajectory outcome (success/failure) backward to label earlier windows. For windows near the end of failed trajectories, this is a reasonable retrospective signal. But for the `exit_context` case (line 166-167 of labeling_functions.py), it labels ALL windows as stagnation regardless of position, and trajectories that exit due to context limits may still contain productive early windows. More critically, since the probe is trained on these backward-looking labels, there is a risk that the temporal model learns to exploit trajectory-position features as a proxy for outcome, rather than genuine window-level progress signals. The cross-agent transfer results partially mitigate this concern but do not fully resolve it.\n\n**W5. The human gold set is too small for robust evaluation.** 400 windows with 156 stagnation and 244 progress is a modest sample. At 0.862 macro-F1, the 95% confidence interval on this metric is roughly +/-3-4pp (bootstrap estimate), which means the observed +2.11pp advantage over Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot (Table 1) may not be significant. The paper does not report confidence intervals for any gold-set results. The early Phase 0.5 quality check (decisions D004-D006) noted that excluding \"uncertain\" samples was needed to reach 81.5% agreement, and the original raw agreement was 53% -- this history raises questions about whether the final 400-window sample was selected for agreeable examples.\n\n**W6. Repeat-3 is a straw-man implementation of cycle detection.** The actual Repeat-3 baseline (baselines.py) checks exact string match of the last 3 AI commands extracted via regex. Real cycle detectors in the literature (CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD) use embedding similarity, n-gram analysis, or learned anomaly detection. The paper cites these methods in Related Work but does not compare against them. The gap between Repeat-3 (exact match) and the LF command_repetition (which uses SequenceMatcher with 0.9 threshold) suggests a soft-match version of Repeat-3 would close part of the gap.\n\n**W7. Cross-agent \"zero-shot transfer\" relies on LF-generated labels for evaluation.** The OpenHands evaluation (48,352 windows) uses the same LFs to generate ground-truth labels, requiring \"format adaptation\" of the command_repetition LF. Since the LFs were designed for SWE-agent, their accuracy on OpenHands is unvalidated. Without human labels on OpenHands trajectories, the cross-agent F1 of 0.835 may partly reflect the probe's ability to mimic adapted LFs rather than genuine progress detection. Even a small human-annotated set on OpenHands (e.g., 100 windows) would substantially strengthen this claim.\n\n**W8. Class imbalance and its interaction with majority vote.** The data distribution is 71.5% progress / 28.5% stagnation. The majority-vote labels have a known bias: 38/400 errors are stagnation-as-progress versus 24/400 progress-as-stagnation, meaning the MV systematically under-labels stagnation. The pos_weight correction in training partially addresses this, but the evaluation metric (macro-F1) can mask systematic class-level biases. Per-class precision and recall are not reported for most tables.\n\n### 4. Questions to Authors\n\n**Q1.** What is the frozen sentence encoder? Why was Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B chosen over alternatives? How sensitive are results to this choice? (This is a critical reproducibility gap.)\n\n**Q2.** The ProgressProbe class in the codebase uses `hidden[-1]` (last GRU hidden state) with no attention. The paper describes BiGRU + attention with learned weight computation. Which model produced the reported numbers? If the attention was added later, are the quantitative results in Tables 1-7 from the attention model or the simpler one?\n\n**Q3.** For the multi-seed experiment (probe-multiseed in experiment_plan.yaml), what were the results? Were they run? If not, can you report mean and standard deviation across at least 3 seeds for the main result?\n\n**Q4.** How were the 400 gold-set windows sampled? Were they stratified by confidence level, trajectory outcome, or difficulty? Were any windows excluded for annotator disagreement during the annotation process?\n\n**Q5.** The early termination simulation (Table 7) reports \"optimal threshold 0.6\" -- optimal by what criterion? Expected cost? Who chose the cost model, and is it sensitive to the assumed cost of a false termination vs. a missed recovery?\n\n**Q6.** For the LLM-as-Judge comparison, what prompt template was used? Was it optimized? How many shots were provided in the few-shot setting, and were they selected from the training set?\n\n**Q7.** The outcome_backpropagation LF labels all windows of `exit_context` trajectories as stagnation. What fraction of the training data comes from `exit_context` trajectories? Could the probe simply learn \"long trajectory = stagnation\"?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- Section 3.3: \"1024\" dimension is stated without naming the encoder. This should be a named model with a citation.\n- Table 4 (LLM-as-Judge): Evaluated on a \"500-window sample\" -- is this the same as or overlapping with the 400-window gold set? If different, why a different sample?\n- The paper claims \"first to apply data programming to sequential agent trajectory data\" (Section 2) -- this should be softened to \"to our knowledge, the first\" (which is done in one place but not the other).\n- The bibliography has several incomplete entries (e.g., \"Luo et al.\" and \"Chen et al.\" with \"and others\" instead of full author lists). Some cited papers may need verification for existence.\n- The Cosine Similarity baseline achieves 0.313 macro-F1, which is below random (0.496). The decisions.yaml flags this as suspicious (\"低于 Random 不正常\") but no investigation outcome is reported in the paper.\n- The paper does not discuss computational cost of the LF pipeline itself (running 3 LFs over 54K windows), which is relevant for the practical framing.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Soundness** | 3/5 |\n| **Presentation** | 4/5 |\n| **Contribution** | 3/5 |\n| **Overall** | 3/6 (Weak Reject) |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 |\n\n### Justification\n\nThe paper tackles a well-motivated problem and proposes a sensible methodology. The experimental coverage is impressively thorough -- 16 experiments covering nearly every angle a reviewer would ask about. The paper is generally well-written with honest framing of the architecture as engineering rather than novelty.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent acceptance at this time. The most serious is the discrepancy between the described model (BiGRU + attention) and the implemented model (unidirectional GRU, last hidden state, no attention), which undermines the integrity of reported results, particularly the attention-based interpretability claims. The unnamed sentence encoder is a basic reproducibility failure. The absence of multi-seed variance reporting makes it impossible to judge statistical reliability of the claimed margins. The human gold set is too small to establish significance on the narrower gaps (e.g., +2.11pp over Claude Sonnet 4). Cross-agent transfer claims rest on unvalidated LF-generated labels for OpenHands.\n\nThese are fixable issues. If the authors (a) clarify and align the code with the described model, (b) name and ablate the embedding model, (c) report multi-seed variance, and (d) add even a small human-annotated set for OpenHands, the paper would be substantially stronger. In its current form, the gap between claims and evidence is too wide for acceptance."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a05975cf0324f7cc2 (use SendMessage with to: 'a05975cf0324f7cc2' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 102762\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 148335</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37dd1f71-0479-4eaf-8dec-0b477d2b7a27","uuid":"ad33db64-8bc8-41d4-a18f-a11acb75a618","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:53:52.139Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate the paper \"ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\" using the 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; acceptance threshold ~25%).\n\n## Paper Summary\nThe paper proposes ProgressProbe, a framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories (agents executing diverse but collectively ineffective actions). Two contributions:\n1. A data-programming pipeline transferring Snorkel to sequential agent trajectories, using 3 labeling functions (file_diff_delta, command_repetition, outcome_backpropagation) to produce window-level progress/stagnation labels via majority vote.\n2. A lightweight GRU probe with attention (1.32M params, ~12 min training) that learns temporal progress signals from these noisy labels.\n\n## Key Results (from paper)\n- **Human gold set (400 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.724)**: Probe 0.862 macro-F1, beating Repeat-3 by +6.89pp, LF Oracle by +4.36pp, Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot by +2.11pp (Table 1)\n- **MV label quality**: 84.5% accuracy vs gold, κ=0.724 (Table 2)\n- **MV labels**: conf≥2 probe 0.906 F1, full data 0.874 F1, both >6pp over Repeat-3 (Table 3)\n- **LLM-as-Judge**: Probe beats Claude S4 few-shot by +3.74pp at 31x lower cost, 606x lower latency (Table 4)\n- **Temporal vs static**: GRU 0.906 vs Mean-pool+LR 0.768, gap=13.72pp; stagnation F1 0.859 vs 0.657 (Table 5)\n- **Cross-agent transfer**: SWE-agent→OpenHands zero-shot 0.835 F1, -7.08pp from within-agent but still +4.53pp over Repeat-3 (Table 6)\n- **Early termination**: Optimal threshold 0.6 saves 32.7% tokens, preserves 96.1% success; intervention paradox: 17.3% terminated trajectories would have resolved but at 2,847 extra tokens (Table 7)\n- **Error analysis**: 221 errors (7.2%), FP=98 (exploratory behavior), FN=123 (diverse ineffective cmds); probe uniquely corrects 187 cases vs Repeat-3 (Tables 8-9)\n- **LF temporal dynamics**: cmd_rep leads by 1.8 steps, file_diff coincident, outcome_bp lags 2.1 steps; onset agreement 34%→peak 71% (Table 10)\n- **Ablations**: Window k=5 sweet spot (Table 11); GRU+Att vs Transformer differ by 0.88pp (Table 12); LF hold-one-out shows all contribute, file_diff most at -4.21pp (Table 13); SupCon +1.31pp (Table 14); Non-NLP features only 0.723 F1 (Table 15); Data scaling saturates ~60% (Table 16)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe review_context.yaml lists 16 imagined experiments. The paper covers all 16. Key experiments:\n- Full-data generalization (conf≥1 vs conf≥2)\n- LF Oracle baseline\n- LLM-as-Judge (Claude S4 + GPT-4o, zero/few-shot)\n- Mean-pool+LR/SVM baselines\n- Error analysis by pattern type\n- Cross-agent transfer (SWE-agent→OpenHands, 48,352 windows)\n- Window size ablation (k=3,5,7)\n- Architecture ablation (GRU+Att, Transformer, GRU+Mean-pool, MLP)\n- LF hold-one-out\n- SupCon ablation\n- Non-NLP baseline (18 structural features)\n- Data scaling (10/25/50/100%)\n- Early termination simulation\n- Interpretability (t-SNE, attention weights)\n- Human-annotated gold set (400 windows, 3 annotators)\n- LF temporal dynamics\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (methods + experiments focus, numbered)\n3. **Weaknesses** (methods + experiments focus, numbered)\n4. **Questions to Authors** (numbered)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-5), Presentation (1-5), Contribution (1-5), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Focus on:\n- Is the experimental methodology sound? Any circularity concerns?\n- Are baselines comprehensive and fairly compared?\n- Is the GRU probe architecture well-motivated vs alternatives?\n- Are the claims well-supported by evidence?\n- Statistical rigor (variance, significance tests, multiple runs)?\n- Data leakage or confounding concerns?\n- Reproducibility\n\nNote: The frozen sentence encoder used for embeddings is not named in the paper — flag this.\n","agentId":"a05975cf0324f7cc2","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, its methodology, implementation details, and experimental design. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories -- situations where agents execute diverse but collectively ineffective actions. The authors propose ProgressProbe, consisting of (1) a data-programming pipeline with three labeling functions (file_diff_delta, command_repetition, outcome_backpropagation) that produce window-level progress/stagnation labels via majority vote, and (2) a lightweight GRU probe with attention (1.32M params) trained on these weakly-supervised labels. The paper reports strong results on SWE-bench trajectories: 0.906 macro-F1 on majority-vote labels, 0.862 on a 400-window human gold set, with cross-agent transfer to OpenHands (0.835 F1) and practical downstream value in early termination (32.7% token savings at 96.1% success preservation).\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Comprehensive experimental coverage.** The paper covers an unusually thorough set of experiments for a methods paper: 16 distinct experiments spanning main results, multiple baseline families, ablations across every design axis (window size, architecture, LF composition, loss function, feature type, data scale), error analysis, cross-agent transfer, and a downstream application. This leaves very few obvious experimental gaps.\n\n**S2. Circularity explicitly addressed.** The most obvious threat to validity -- that the probe is trained and evaluated on the same automatically generated labels -- is directly mitigated by the 400-window human-annotated gold set (Fleiss kappa=0.724, substantial agreement). The probe's advantage over baselines holds on human labels (0.862 macro-F1, +6.89pp over Repeat-3), which is the right evaluation to foreground. The paper correctly places this as Table 1.\n\n**S3. Strong baseline suite that includes LLM-as-Judge.** Including Claude Sonnet 4 and GPT-4o in zero-shot and few-shot settings preempts the obvious \"why not just use an LLM?\" question. The cost-accuracy Pareto analysis (31x cheaper, 606x faster, AND higher F1) is convincing. The static aggregation baselines (Mean-pool+LR/SVM, Max-pool+LR) provide clean controls for isolating the contribution of temporal modeling.\n\n**S4. Instance-level data splitting.** The code confirms that train/val/test splits are done by instance_id (not by window), preventing data leakage from windows of the same trajectory appearing in both train and test. This is a critical correctness property that many papers in this space miss.\n\n**S5. Principled separation of method from architecture.** The paper explicitly frames the GRU architecture as a \"deliberate engineering choice, not a contribution\" and supports this with ablations showing GRU and Transformer differ by <1pp. This honest positioning strengthens the methodological contribution (weak-supervision pipeline + empirical study).\n\n**S6. Informative error analysis.** The decomposition into FP (exploratory behavior misclassified as stagnation, 98 cases) and FN (diverse but ineffective commands, 123 cases) identifies meaningful failure modes. The error overlap analysis with Repeat-3 (probe uniquely corrects 187 cases, loses only 34) quantifies the practical advantage precisely.\n\n**S7. LF temporal dynamics analysis.** The observation that labeling functions exhibit leading/lagging behavior at stagnation onset (cmd_rep leads by 1.8 steps, outcome_bp lags by 2.1 steps, agreement rises from 34% to 71%) is a genuinely novel insight about applying data programming to sequential data. This is the most original analytical contribution.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The sentence encoder is not named in the paper.** Section 3.3 says only \"a frozen sentence encoder\" with dimension 1024. The source code reveals this is **Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B** with last-token pooling and L2 normalization, truncated to 512 tokens. This is a serious omission for reproducibility. The choice of embedding model is a critical design decision -- results may differ substantially with, e.g., all-MiniLM-L6 or BGE-large. An embedding model ablation is entirely absent.\n\n**W2. No variance reporting or significance testing across multiple runs.** The experiment plan includes a \"probe-multiseed\" experiment (seeds 42, 123, 456) to confirm the 6.24pp gap is statistically stable, but the paper reports only single-seed results. With margins of 3-7pp over baselines and a relatively small human gold set (400 windows), it is impossible to assess whether the observed gains are within run-to-run variance. This is a standard requirement for ML venues. The training code sets seed=42 with no multi-run support built in.\n\n**W3. The GRU probe lacks the attention mechanism described in the paper.** The paper describes a BiGRU with attention (Equations 2-3, Section 3.4), complete with attention weight analysis (concentration coefficient 0.73, onset localization at steps 2-3). However, the actual `ProgressProbe` class in `probe_model.py` implements a **unidirectional 2-layer GRU that takes the last hidden state** (`hidden[-1]`) followed by a linear classifier -- there is no attention mechanism whatsoever. Either the paper describes a different model than what was actually trained, or the code reviewed here is not the final version. This discrepancy undermines trust in the reported attention-based interpretability results.\n\n**W4. outcome_backpropagation LF introduces label leakage from the future.** This LF propagates the final trajectory outcome (success/failure) backward to label earlier windows. For windows near the end of failed trajectories, this is a reasonable retrospective signal. But for the `exit_context` case (line 166-167 of labeling_functions.py), it labels ALL windows as stagnation regardless of position, and trajectories that exit due to context limits may still contain productive early windows. More critically, since the probe is trained on these backward-looking labels, there is a risk that the temporal model learns to exploit trajectory-position features as a proxy for outcome, rather than genuine window-level progress signals. The cross-agent transfer results partially mitigate this concern but do not fully resolve it.\n\n**W5. The human gold set is too small for robust evaluation.** 400 windows with 156 stagnation and 244 progress is a modest sample. At 0.862 macro-F1, the 95% confidence interval on this metric is roughly +/-3-4pp (bootstrap estimate), which means the observed +2.11pp advantage over Claude Sonnet 4 few-shot (Table 1) may not be significant. The paper does not report confidence intervals for any gold-set results. The early Phase 0.5 quality check (decisions D004-D006) noted that excluding \"uncertain\" samples was needed to reach 81.5% agreement, and the original raw agreement was 53% -- this history raises questions about whether the final 400-window sample was selected for agreeable examples.\n\n**W6. Repeat-3 is a straw-man implementation of cycle detection.** The actual Repeat-3 baseline (baselines.py) checks exact string match of the last 3 AI commands extracted via regex. Real cycle detectors in the literature (CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD) use embedding similarity, n-gram analysis, or learned anomaly detection. The paper cites these methods in Related Work but does not compare against them. The gap between Repeat-3 (exact match) and the LF command_repetition (which uses SequenceMatcher with 0.9 threshold) suggests a soft-match version of Repeat-3 would close part of the gap.\n\n**W7. Cross-agent \"zero-shot transfer\" relies on LF-generated labels for evaluation.** The OpenHands evaluation (48,352 windows) uses the same LFs to generate ground-truth labels, requiring \"format adaptation\" of the command_repetition LF. Since the LFs were designed for SWE-agent, their accuracy on OpenHands is unvalidated. Without