{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JJ7gRxui1Wspo3RMFdAQwS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**R1 Response to R2's Review**\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree with nearly all of R2's weaknesses. W2 (incomplete related work) is a significant oversight I should have flagged more strongly -- the absence of PRM literature (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd) is particularly glaring since ProgressProbe is functionally a process reward model trained via weak supervision rather than human annotation. W1 (thin novelty) aligns with my overall impression: the technical pipeline is Snorkel + GRU, both off-the-shelf. W5-W6 (domain specificity) reinforces my concern about generalizability -- two agents on one benchmark is insufficient.\n\nR2's Q6 (LR/RF on handcrafted features) is an excellent question I missed. If a simple logistic regression on features like token count, edit frequency, and error rate approaches GRU performance, the learned representation story weakens considerably.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nW3 (semantic stagnation as rebrand) is partially unfair. While the concept overlaps with exploration-exploitation failure, the operationalization via embedding similarity over sliding windows is concrete and measurable, not merely a relabeling. The labeling function itself may have issues (my W4 on future leakage), but the concept has independent value.\n\nW4 (LLM-as-judge underspecified) is valid but minor -- the paper positions LLM-as-judge as a cost baseline, not a competing method. One prompt template is fine for that purpose.\n\nI also think R2 underweights S2 (student exceeds teacher). The +4.43pp over LF oracle is genuinely interesting and deserves more investigation, not dismissal.\n\n**Impact on My Assessment**\n\nR2's review reinforces my concerns rather than changing them. The related work gap (R2-W2) and the PRM framing question are serious enough that they should have been in my review. However, R2's novelty critique is harsher than mine -- I see the weak supervision angle as having some value if the execution were cleaner.\n\n**Updated Score: 3/6 (unchanged).** The paper needs architectural honesty (my W3), variance reporting (W2), expanded related work (R2-W2), and multi-domain evaluation before acceptance."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ac41e2ede1c75228a (use SendMessage with to: 'ac41e2ede1c75228a' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15279\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 18155</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37dd1f71-0479-4eaf-8dec-0b477d2b7a27","uuid":"35ac8f71-9e83-4af7-9b8b-c6c9780d01bd","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:54:31.752Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1, Methods + Experiments focus). You previously reviewed the paper \"ProgressProbe\" and gave it 3/6 (Weak Reject). Your key concerns were:\n- W1: Sentence encoder unnamed (Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B per code, no ablation)\n- W2: No variance/significance across multiple runs\n- W3: Code-paper discrepancy (code has unidirectional GRU with last hidden state, no attention; paper describes BiGRU+attention)\n- W4: outcome_backpropagation LF leaks future information\n- W5: Human gold set too small (400 windows)\n- W6: Repeat-3 is a straw-man (exact match only)\n- W7: Cross-agent evaluation uses unvalidated LF-generated labels\n- W8: Class imbalance and MV bias\n\nNow read Reviewer 2's review (Novelty + Related Work focus), who also gave 3/6 (Reject):\n\n---\n**R2's Key Strengths:**\n- S1: Problem identification practically valuable\n- S2: \"Student exceeds teacher\" effect (+4.43pp over LF oracle) is cleanest conceptual contribution\n- S3: Cross-agent transfer meaningful\n- S4: Cost-efficiency comparison well-grounded\n\n**R2's Key Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Novelty thin — applying Snorkel + training GRU are both straightforward\n- W2: Related work severely incomplete (10 refs). Missing: Process Reward Models (Lightman 2023, Math-Shepherd), RL value functions, Reflexion/LATS agent scaffolding, execution feedback, trajectory anomaly detection beyond cited works, weak supervision beyond Snorkel\n- W3: \"Semantic stagnation\" may rebrand existing concepts (exploration-exploitation failure)\n- W4: LLM-as-judge comparison underspecified (one prompt template)\n- W5: Domain specificity (SWE-bench only) undermines generality\n- W6: Evaluation confined to single task domain (both agents on SWE-bench)\n\n**R2's Scores:** Soundness 3, Presentation 3, Contribution 2, Overall 3/6, Confidence 4\n\n**R2's Questions:**\n1. How does this differ from training a PRM on agent trajectories?\n2. Could TD learning achieve similar results?\n3. What's the plan for non-coding domains?\n4. Why only 10 references?\n5. What embedder is used?\n6. Was LR/RF on handcrafted features tried?\n---\n\nYour task: Write a **brief** (under 300 words) response addressing:\n1. Which of R2's points do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Does R2's review change your overall assessment? If so, how?\n4. What is your updated score (if any)?\n","agentId":"ac41e2ede1c75228a","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**R1 Response to R2's Review**\n\n**Points of Agreement**\n\nI agree with nearly all of R2's weaknesses. W2 (incomplete related work) is a significant oversight I should have flagged more strongly -- the absence of PRM literature (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd) is particularly glaring since ProgressProbe is functionally a process reward model trained via weak supervision rather than human annotation. W1 (thin novelty) aligns with my overall impression: the technical pipeline is Snorkel + GRU, both off-the-shelf. W5-W6 (domain specificity) reinforces my concern about generalizability -- two agents on one benchmark is insufficient.\n\nR2's Q6 (LR/RF on handcrafted features) is an excellent question I missed. If a simple logistic regression on features like token count, edit frequency, and error rate approaches GRU performance, the learned representation story weakens considerably.\n\n**Points of Disagreement**\n\nW3 (semantic stagnation as rebrand) is partially unfair. While the concept overlaps with exploration-exploitation failure, the operationalization via embedding similarity over sliding windows is concrete and measurable, not merely a relabeling. The labeling function itself may have issues (my W4 on future leakage), but the concept has independent value.\n\nW4 (LLM-as-judge underspecified) is valid but minor -- the paper positions LLM-as-judge as a cost baseline, not a competing method. One prompt template is fine for that purpose.\n\nI also think R2 underweights S2 (student exceeds teacher). The +4.43pp over LF oracle is genuinely interesting and deserves more investigation, not dismissal.\n\n**Impact on My Assessment**\n\nR2's review reinforces my concerns rather than changing them. The related work gap (R2-W2) and the PRM framing question are serious enough that they should have been in my review. However, R2's novelty critique is harsher than mine -- I see the weak supervision angle as having some value if the execution were cleaner.\n\n**Updated Score: 3/6 (unchanged).** The paper needs architectural honesty (my W3), variance reporting (W2), expanded related work (R2-W2), and multi-domain evaluation before acceptance."}],"totalDurationMs":18155,"totalTokens":15279,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6502,"cache_read_input_tokens":8247,"output_tokens":528,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6502},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":528,"cache_read_input_tokens":8247,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6502,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6502,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
