{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016oq2ziPUsVPnQ1CjNBkCqi","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n**Points of agreement:**\n\nR1's W3 (code-paper discrepancy) is the most damaging finding in either review. If the reported model is a unidirectional 2-layer GRU with last-hidden-state pooling while the paper describes BiGRU+attention with learned weights, then the attention concentration coefficient (0.73) and onset localization claims are unsupported by the actual artifact. This alone warrants rejection. I fully agree with W1 (unnamed encoder), W2 (single seed), W4 (outcome_backpropagation leakage), and W5 (small gold set). These reinforce my own concerns about evaluation rigor.\n\nR1's W6 (Repeat-3 as straw-man) and W8 (class imbalance bias) are valid but secondary — they weaken the empirical narrative without being fatal individually.\n\n**Points of disagreement:**\n\nR1 gives Presentation 4/5, which I find generous given the code-paper discrepancy they themselves uncovered. A paper that describes an architecture different from its implementation has a presentation problem, not just a soundness problem. I would score Presentation at 2-3.\n\nI also think R1 underweights S7 (\"LF temporal dynamics is genuinely novel\"). The temporal signal from labeling functions is an interesting observation, but it is not developed into a methodological contribution — it remains a descriptive finding. Calling it \"genuinely novel\" overstates its current form.\n\n**Impact on my assessment:**\n\nR1's findings strengthen my rejection. My W1 (novelty thin) is now compounded by evidence that even the modest architectural claims (BiGRU+attention) may not reflect what was actually evaluated. My W3 (\"semantic stagnation\" rebranding) gains further weight: if the attention mechanism that supposedly identifies stagnation patterns does not exist in the code, the interpretability story collapses entirely.\n\n**Updated score:** 2/6 (Strong Reject), down from 3/6. The code-paper discrepancy transforms concerns about thin novelty into concerns about reproducibility and accuracy of reported results. Confidence: 4."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4dbdae2bd0c1d696 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4dbdae2bd0c1d696' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16603\ntool_uses: 1\nduration_ms: 18732</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37dd1f71-0479-4eaf-8dec-0b477d2b7a27","uuid":"afa90b61-df77-4f90-93b8-a0755c4c982c","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:54:44.488Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2, Novelty + Related Work focus). You previously reviewed the paper \"ProgressProbe\" and gave it 3/6 (Reject). Your key concerns were:\n- W1: Novelty thin (Snorkel + GRU = straightforward)\n- W2: Related work severely incomplete (10 refs, missing PRMs, RL value functions, Reflexion/LATS)\n- W3: \"Semantic stagnation\" may rebrand existing concepts\n- W4: LLM-as-judge comparison underspecified\n- W5: Domain specificity (SWE-bench only)\n- W6: Evaluation confined to single task domain\n\nNow read Reviewer 1's review (Methods + Experiments focus), who gave 3/6 (Weak Reject):\n\n---\n**R1's Key Strengths:**\n- S1: Comprehensive experimental coverage (16 experiments)\n- S2: Circularity addressed via human gold set\n- S3: Strong baseline suite including LLM-as-Judge\n- S4: Instance-level data splitting (no leakage)\n- S5: Honest positioning of architecture as engineering, not contribution\n- S6: Informative error analysis\n- S7: LF temporal dynamics is genuinely novel insight\n\n**R1's Key Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Sentence encoder unnamed (discovered as Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B in code, no ablation)\n- W2: No variance/significance across runs (single seed=42)\n- W3: **Code-paper discrepancy**: code implements unidirectional 2-layer GRU with last hidden state, NO attention mechanism; paper describes BiGRU+attention with learned weights. All attention-based interpretability claims (concentration coefficient 0.73, onset localization) may be fabricated or from a different model.\n- W4: outcome_backpropagation LF introduces future-information leakage\n- W5: Human gold set too small (400 windows, CI ~+/-3-4pp)\n- W6: Repeat-3 is a straw-man (exact string match)\n- W7: Cross-agent evaluation uses unvalidated LF-generated labels for OpenHands\n- W8: Class imbalance + MV systematic bias\n\n**R1's Scores:** Soundness 3, Presentation 4, Contribution 3, Overall 3/6, Confidence 4\n\n**R1's Questions:**\n- Q1: What is the frozen encoder?\n- Q2: Which model produced the reported numbers — attention model or the simpler one?\n- Q3: Multi-seed results?\n- Q4: How were 400 gold windows sampled?\n- Q5: How was early-termination \"optimal threshold\" chosen?\n- Q6: LLM-as-judge prompt details?\n- Q7: What fraction of training data from exit_context trajectories?\n---\n\nYour task: Write a **brief** (under 300 words) response addressing:\n1. Which of R1's points do you agree with?\n2. Which do you disagree with, and why?\n3. Does R1's review change your overall assessment? If so, how?\n4. What is your updated score (if any)?\n","agentId":"a4dbdae2bd0c1d696","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n**Points of agreement:**\n\nR1's W3 (code-paper discrepancy) is the most damaging finding in either review. If the reported model is a unidirectional 2-layer GRU with last-hidden-state pooling while the paper describes BiGRU+attention with learned weights, then the attention concentration coefficient (0.73) and onset localization claims are unsupported by the actual artifact. This alone warrants rejection. I fully agree with W1 (unnamed encoder), W2 (single seed), W4 (outcome_backpropagation leakage), and W5 (small gold set). These reinforce my own concerns about evaluation rigor.\n\nR1's W6 (Repeat-3 as straw-man) and W8 (class imbalance bias) are valid but secondary — they weaken the empirical narrative without being fatal individually.\n\n**Points of disagreement:**\n\nR1 gives Presentation 4/5, which I find generous given the code-paper discrepancy they themselves uncovered. A paper that describes an architecture different from its implementation has a presentation problem, not just a soundness problem. I would score Presentation at 2-3.\n\nI also think R1 underweights S7 (\"LF temporal dynamics is genuinely novel\"). The temporal signal from labeling functions is an interesting observation, but it is not developed into a methodological contribution — it remains a descriptive finding. Calling it \"genuinely novel\" overstates its current form.\n\n**Impact on my assessment:**\n\nR1's findings strengthen my rejection. My W1 (novelty thin) is now compounded by evidence that even the modest architectural claims (BiGRU+attention) may not reflect what was actually evaluated. My W3 (\"semantic stagnation\" rebranding) gains further weight: if the attention mechanism that supposedly identifies stagnation patterns does not exist in the code, the interpretability story collapses entirely.\n\n**Updated score:** 2/6 (Strong Reject), down from 3/6. The code-paper discrepancy transforms concerns about thin novelty into concerns about reproducibility and accuracy of reported results. Confidence: 4."}],"totalDurationMs":18732,"totalTokens":16603,"totalToolUseCount":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1284,"cache_read_input_tokens":14815,"output_tokens":501,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1284},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":501,"cache_read_input_tokens":14815,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1284,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1284,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":0,"searchCount":0,"