{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018tNUJYCQqAe1Q2kZCP3DLG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now let me create a comprehensive summary of my findings. Based on my thorough analysis of the code, I can now provide you with a complete answer about the probe model's architecture.\n\n## Summary: GRU Probe Model Analysis\n\nI have found and analyzed the ProgressProbe implementation in your project. Here are my findings:\n\n**File Locations:**\n- `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/probe_model.py`\n- `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/_project/intermediate/probe_model.py`\n\nBoth files contain identical implementations.\n\n### Key Findings:\n\n**1. GRU Type:**\nThe model uses a **standard unidirectional GRU**, not BiGRU. The definition is:\n```python\nself.gru = nn.GRU(\n    embed_dim,\n    hidden_size,\n    num_layers=num_layers,\n    batch_first=True,\n    dropout=dropout if num_layers > 1 else 0.0,\n)\n```\nThe `bidirectional=False` is implicit (default). There is no bidirectional parameter set.\n\n**2. Attention Mechanism:**\nThe GRU probe **does NOT have an attention mechanism**. There are:\n- No learned attention weights (no `v` or `W` parameters for attention)\n- No attention module or layer\n- No softmax-weighted computation over hidden states\n\n**3. Final Representation:**\nThe model uses the **last hidden state** from the top GRU layer:\n```python\ndef forward(self, x: torch.Tensor, lengths: torch.Tensor = None) -> torch.Tensor:\n    if lengths is not None:\n        x = nn.utils.rnn.pack_padded_sequence(x, lengths.cpu(), batch_first=True, enforce_sorted=False)\n    _, hidden = self.gru(x)\n    out = hidden[-1]  # last layer hidden: (batch, hidden_size)\n    return self.classifier(out).squeeze(-1)\n```\n\nThe line `out = hidden[-1]` extracts the final hidden state from the last layer (index -1), which has shape `(batch, hidden_size)`.\n\n**4. Complete Architecture:**\n- **Input:** `(batch, seq_len, embed_dim)` - variable-length window embeddings\n- **GRU:** 2-layer unidirectional GRU with 256 hidden units and 0.1 dropout (default config)\n- **Classifier:** Single linear layer `nn.Linear(hidden_size, 1)` mapping hidden state to binary logit\n- **Output:** `(batch,)` logits (sigmoid applied during training/evaluation for probability)\n\n**Conclusion:** The GRU probe is a simple 2-layer unidirectional GRU model without attention. It relies entirely on the sequential processing capability of GRU and uses the final hidden state as the summary representation for binary classification (progress vs. stagnation)."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"37dd1f71-0479-4eaf-8dec-0b477d2b7a27","uuid":"a0357919-6110-4a8e-bd85-d821279d2b04","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:55:17.261Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Search the project at /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/ for the probe model implementation. I need to verify whether the GRU probe has an attention mechanism or not.\n\n1. Find files named probe_model.py or similar that define the ProgressProbe class\n2. Read the full model class definition\n3. Check if there is: (a) BiGRU or unidirectional GRU, (b) an attention mechanism with learned weights (v, W parameters), (c) how the final representation is computed (last hidden state vs attention-weighted sum)\n\nReport the exact code found, including class name, forward method, and any attention-related code. Be thorough — check multiple files if needed (e.g., src/, models/, experiments/ directories).\n","agentId":"a6e8e65780b22a590","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect. Now let me create a comprehensive summary of my findings. Based on my thorough analysis of the code, I can now provide you with a complete answer about the probe model's architecture.\n\n## Summary: GRU Probe Model Analysis\n\nI have found and analyzed the ProgressProbe implementation in your project. Here are my findings:\n\n**File Locations:**\n- `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/probe_model.py`\n- `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/_project/intermediate/probe_model.py`\n\nBoth files contain identical implementations.\n\n### Key Findings:\n\n**1. GRU Type:**\nThe model uses a **standard unidirectional GRU**, not BiGRU. The definition is:\n```python\nself.gru = nn.GRU(\n    embed_dim,\n    hidden_size,\n    num_layers=num_layers,\n    batch_first=True,\n    dropout=dropout if num_layers > 1 else 0.0,\n)\n```\nThe `bidirectional=False` is implicit (default). There is no bidirectional parameter set.\n\n**2. Attention Mechanism:**\nThe GRU probe **does NOT have an attention mechanism**. There are:\n- No learned attention weights (no `v` or `W` parameters for attention)\n- No attention module or layer\n- No softmax-weighted computation over hidden states\n\n**3. Final Representation:**\nThe model uses the **last hidden state** from the top GRU layer:\n```python\ndef forward(self, x: torch.Tensor, lengths: torch.Tensor = None) -> torch.Tensor:\n    if lengths is not None:\n        x = nn.utils.rnn.pack_padded_sequence(x, lengths.cpu(), batch_first=True, enforce_sorted=False)\n    _, hidden = self.gru(x)\n    out = hidden[-1]  # last layer hidden: (batch, hidden_size)\n    return self.classifier(out).squeeze(-1)\n```\n\nThe line `out = hidden[-1]` extracts the final hidden state from the last layer (index -1), which has shape `(batch, hidden_size)`.\n\n**4. Complete Architecture:**\n- **Input:** `(batch, seq_len, embed_dim)` - variable-length window embeddings\n- **GRU:** 2-layer unidirectional GRU with 256 hidden units and 0.1 dropout (default config)\n- **Classifier:** Single linear layer `nn.Linear(hidden_size, 1)` mapping hidden state to binary logit\n- **Output:** `(batch,)` logits (sigmoid applied during training/evaluation for probability)\n\n**Conclusion:** The GRU probe is a simple 2-layer unidirectional GRU model without attention. It relies entirely on the sequential processing capability of GRU and uses the final hidden state as the summary representation for binary classification (progress vs. stagnation)."}],"totalDurationMs":20917,"totalTokens":32924,"totalToolUseCount":11,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1375,"cache_read_input_tokens":30820,"output_tokens":725,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1375},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":725,"cache_read_input_tokens":30820,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1375,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1375,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":5,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":6,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
